The Stoics held that everything happens by fate, and also that people are responsible for what they do. Critics from Carneades onward said those two claims cannot both be true. Chrysippus spent much of his career arguing that they can, and Tim O'Keefe calls the Stoics the first clearly compatibilist thinkers in the Western tradition.

This summary draws on the Arete corpus: Cicero's On Fate and On the Nature of the Gods, Epictetus's Discourses, Seneca's On Providence and Letters, Marcus Aurelius, Plutarch's attacks on the Stoics, Pearson's collection of Zeno and Cleanthes, and the scholarship of Arnold, Hicks, Zeller, O'Keefe, Bobzien, Vázquez, and Šuster. It is a companion to Stoic Logic: A Summary, which covers the logical side of this debate in more detail.$q$, 124, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/fate-providence-up-to-us.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
