Three Stoic commitments pull against each other:

1. Fate. Every event, including every human choice, follows from prior causes in an unbroken chain.

2. Providence. That chain is the work of a good and rational god, arranging things for the best.

3. Responsibility. People deserve praise and blame for what they do, and some things are genuinely up to them.

If fate is true, critics asked, why deliberate, why call a doctor, and why blame anyone? If providence is true, why do good people suffer? The Stoic answers to those questions are the subject of what follows.$q$, 97, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/fate-providence-up-to-us.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$Fate, Providence, and What Is Up to Us: A Summary$q$, $q$Fate: the chain of causes$q$, 1003, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
