For the Stoics, fate is not a blind force but the rational order of the world seen as a sequence of causes. Cicero gives the standard definition: fate is the order and series of causes, each linked to the next and producing its effect (On Divination, via Arnold).

Chrysippus, Posidonius, and Zeno all held that everything happens by fate (Arnold). Seneca called it the necessity of all things and actions, which no force can break (Arnold).

The Stoics gave it several names, which is itself revealing. Zeno called fate a power that moves matter by fixed laws, and said it could equally be called providence or nature. Chrysippus called it a spiritual force ordering the universe, the reason (logos) of the cosmos, and the law by which past things have happened, present things happen, and future things will happen (Arnold).

The doctrine rests on Stoic physics. The cosmos is a single living body pervaded by a divine fire or breath, and only bodies can act or be acted on. There is no motion without a cause, so each effect becomes a cause in turn. Arnold draws the conclusion the Stoics drew: whoever knew every cause could trace every consequence.

Chrysippus also argued for fate from logic. Every proposition is either true or false. A motion with no cause would make some propositions about it neither true nor false. So every motion has a cause, and everything happens by fate (Cicero, On Fate). This is why Chrysippus fought so hard to prove that every proposition is true or false, and why Epicurus, to escape fate, was willing to deny it.$q$, 269, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/fate-providence-up-to-us.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$Fate, Providence, and What Is Up to Us: A Summary$q$, $q$Providence and god$q$, 1004, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
