Verification: corpus_verified: written from and checked against passages in the Arete corpus.
Citing: cite the ancient or scholarly source this passage names, not this summary. Where it names none, say the point comes from an Arete synthesis.

The ancient debate over fate was fought as much in logic as in physics, and Cicero's On Fate is the main surviving record. Three rival positions shaped it.

|Thinker|Position|Consequence|
|---|---|---|
|Diodorus|Only what is or will be true is possible|Whatever will not happen is impossible; the future is as fixed as the past|
|Chrysippus|Every proposition is true or false, and every event has a cause, but some things are possible that never happen|Fate without necessity: the breakable gem that is never broken, the oracle about Cypselus that could have failed|
|Epicurus|To escape fate, deny that every proposition about the future is true or false, and let atoms swerve without a cause|Freedom bought with uncaused motion, which Cicero calls absurd|

Cicero presses Chrysippus with an astrologer's rule: if anyone is born at the rising of the dog star, he will not die at sea. If that conditional is true and the birth is past, and past truths are necessary, then Fabius's not dying at sea looks necessary too, which is Diodorus's view. Chrysippus's escape was to have the astrologers restate their rules as negated conjunctions: it is not the case that someone is born under the dog star and dies at sea. Cicero found the suggestion ridiculous and asked whether doctors and geometers must rephrase their knowledge the same way (On Fate).

Carneades cut deeper. He argued that the Epicureans did not need the swerve at all: the soul's voluntary motion is caused by its own nature, not by anything outside, so it is caused without being fated. On his account, even Apollo could not foresee what has no cause already present in nature (On Fate). Cicero himself, in an Academic mood, said he would sooner deny that every proposition is true or false than grant that fate governs everything. The logical side of this dispute, including Diodorus's Master Argument, is covered in Stoic Logic: A Summary.$q$, 314, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/fate-providence-up-to-us.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$Fate, Providence, and What Is Up to Us: A Summary$q$, $q$The Lazy Argument and co-fated events$q$, 1007, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
