The most practical objection to fate was the "lazy argument" (argos logos). Cicero states it this way: if it is fated that you recover from this illness, you will recover whether or not you call a doctor; if it is fated that you do not, you will not recover either way; so calling a doctor is pointless (On Fate). Taken seriously, it would justify doing nothing at all.

Chrysippus answered with the idea of "co-fated" events (confatalia). Some fated facts are simple, like "Socrates will die on such a day," which will happen whatever he does. Others are joined: "Laius will beget Oedipus" cannot be fated apart from Laius lying with his wife, and "Milo will wrestle at Olympia" cannot be fated apart from an opponent. Recovery and calling the doctor are fated together (On Fate). Arnold adds an example from Homer: soldiers told to keep watch cannot reason that the ships are safe if fate protects them, because there is no such thing as a watch kept by sleepers or a victory won by those who run.

O'Keefe notes the limit of this reply. It shows that our actions matter as causes, but it does not yet show that we are responsible for them. That needed a further argument.$q$, 210, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/fate-providence-up-to-us.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$Fate, Providence, and What Is Up to Us: A Summary$q$, $q$Causes and the cylinder$q$, 1008, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
Document: Fate, Providence, and What Is Up to Us: A Summary (version 1). Section: Causes and the cylinder.
Verification: via_summary: checked against the corpus, but the corpus source is itself a summary of a book or paper, not the text.
