Chrysippus's central move was to distinguish kinds of cause. Cicero reports that he separated "absolute principal" causes from "auxiliary proximate" ones. When the Stoics say everything happens by prior causes, they mean the second kind: causes that start something off without fully determining what it becomes (On Fate).

His illustration is the cylinder. Push a cylinder and you give it the start of its motion, but not its rolling; it rolls because it is a cylinder. The example is usually paired with a cone, which pushed the same way moves differently because of its own shape (Vázquez; Šuster). In the same way, an impression strikes the mind from outside, but how the mind responds, whether it assents, depends on the mind's own nature (On Fate).

Scholars in the corpus read the analogy in similar terms. O'Keefe: impressions trigger action, but how one responds is a function of the person one is. Vázquez: the external impression is not enough to settle the character of the effect, because assent lies with the agent's commanding faculty. Šuster classes the cylinder as an explanatory analogy rather than a proof, showing the priority of the agent's inner nature within the total set of causes, and defends it against Alexander of Aphrodisias's objection that it would apply just as well to animals and objects.

Cicero concluded that, properly explained, Chrysippus and his opponents differed more in words than in substance: both agreed that some effects follow from causes we cannot prevent, while others, though caused, remain ours to shape. Cicero also judged that Chrysippus, while trying to take a middle path, slid back toward necessity by his own language (On Fate).$q$, 275, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/fate-providence-up-to-us.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$via_summary$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$Fate, Providence, and What Is Up to Us: A Summary$q$, $q$What is up to us$q$, 1009, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
