The phrase eph' hēmin, "up to us," names what the Stoics saved from fate. It is not the ability to have done otherwise, but the fact that assent comes from us. O'Keefe explains that in rational animals every real action requires assent to an impression about how to act, and because assent flows from the agent's own reason and character rather than outside force, the action is truly the agent's.

Epictetus made this the center of his teaching. Education, he says, means learning to apply our general notions of good and bad to particular cases, and learning to distinguish what is in our power from what is not. In our power are the will and everything that depends on it; not in our power are the body, possessions, parents, children, country, and everyone we live among (Discourses I.22). O'Keefe notes Epictetus's claim that the correct use of impressions is the one thing god placed entirely under our control.

Freedom follows from this. The free person is the one who cannot be restrained, and the only way never to be restrained is to desire nothing that belongs to others, meaning nothing outside our own will (Discourses IV.1).$q$, 196, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/fate-providence-up-to-us.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$via_summary$q$]::text[], true),
