The Stoics did not think responsibility requires that you could have done otherwise. O'Keefe calls this their rejection of the principle of alternate possibilities, and reconstructs two arguments for it.

The ethical argument. The virtuous person cannot act viciously; virtue is a reliable skill that rules it out. If praise required the ability to do otherwise, the best people would be the least praiseworthy, which is absurd. Seneca says the good man cannot fail to do right, not because he is forced but because he cannot want otherwise (O'Keefe).

The metaphysical argument. If a person in exactly the same inner and outer circumstances could act differently on different occasions, the difference would have no cause. That would make action random, not free. On this view, Carneades's self-moving will only moves the gap instead of closing it (O'Keefe).$q$, 137, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/fate-providence-up-to-us.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$via_summary$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$Fate, Providence, and What Is Up to Us: A Summary$q$, $q$Following willingly$q$, 1011, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
