The practical side of Stoic fate is less an argument than an attitude: accept what happens as part of a rational whole, and make your will agree with it. Pearson calls this the true Stoic answer to the charge that providence rules out freedom.

Cleanthes's prayer. The best known expression is a short poem by Cleanthes, preserved by Epictetus (Handbook 53). In G. H. Rendall's translation, quoted by Arnold:

Lead me, O Zeus, and lead me, Destiny, What way soe'er ye have appointed me! I follow unafraid: yea, though the will Turn recreant, I needs must follow still.

Seneca translated the lines into Latin and added one of his own that became a proverb: the fates lead the willing and drag the unwilling (Letters 107; Pearson; Hicks).

The dog and the cart. The same idea appears as an image reported by Hippolytus. A dog tied to a cart follows willingly if it chooses, and is dragged if it does not; either way it goes where the cart goes. So with people: those who will not follow are compelled all the same (Zeller). The attribution is disputed. Vázquez notes it is credited to Zeno and may mean that only large outward events are fated, not inner attitudes; Šuster reports that Bobzien doubts it is Chrysippean at all; and O'Keefe warns against reading it as fatalism, since it would make god override human resistance, which does not fit Chrysippus's view.

Epictetus. Epictetus returns to Cleanthes's lines again and again. Keep the words ready, he tells a student: if it is god's will that I go to Rome, I go to Rome; to the island of Gyara, to Gyara; to prison, to prison (Discourses IV.4). He treats logic and eloquence as inns on the road home, pleasant places to pass through, while the real purpose is to use impressions rightly and be able to say those lines with your whole soul (Discourses II.23).

Marcus Aurelius. Marcus often draws the contrast between what is up to us and what is not. Pearson points to his remark that only a rational being is given the power to follow events willingly, while merely following is compulsory for everything (Meditations X.28). Zeller adds Marcus's thought that even the person who complains and resists still does work the universe needed.$q$, 382, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/fate-providence-up-to-us.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$Fate, Providence, and What Is Up to Us: A Summary$q$, $q$Critics and verdicts$q$, 1012, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
Document: Fate, Providence, and What Is Up to Us: A Summary (version 1). Section: Critics and verdicts.
Verification: via_summary: checked against the corpus, but the corpus source is itself a summary of a book or paper, not the text.
