The Stoic position drew fire from every side, and much of what we know of it survives inside the attacks.

|Critic|Objection|
|---|---|
|Carneades|If everything happens by prior causes, nothing is up to us; but some things are up to us; so not everything happens by fate (Cicero, On Fate)|
|Cicero|Chrysippus's distinction of causes ends up affirming necessity in spite of itself; his advice to astrologers is a verbal trick|
|Epicurus|Rejected fate by making atoms swerve without cause, a move Cicero thought worse than the disease|
|Alexander of Aphrodisias|The cylinder model fits a stone as well as a person, so it cannot capture human agency (via Šuster)|
|Plutarch|Collected Chrysippus's statements on fate, god, and evil as proof that he contradicted himself (via Arnold)|
|Kant|Dismissed this kind of compatibilism as a wretched subterfuge (via Šuster)|

Modern scholarship is kinder. O'Keefe treats the Stoics as the founders of compatibilism, with a worked-out theory of action and an argument that responsibility does not need alternative possibilities. Bobzien's modal logic shows how Chrysippus's definition of possibility, cut between Philo and Diodorus, made room for unrealized possibilities and so for deliberation, while noting that possibility alone does not make something up to us. Arnold, writing in 1911, judged that the many Stoic answers on evil betrayed a weak case, but admired the ethics that grew from the concession that god works within necessity.$q$, 226, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/fate-providence-up-to-us.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$via_summary$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$Fate, Providence, and What Is Up to Us: A Summary$q$, $q$Where the evidence runs thin$q$, 1013, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
