Chrysippus wrote at length on fate, providence, and possibility, and none of it survives whole. Our main primary source, Cicero's On Fate, is written by a critic and has lost both its beginning and its end. Alexander's On Fate and Gellius's quotations of Chrysippus are not in the corpus and reach this summary through Arnold and Zeller. Plutarch's On Stoic Self-Contradictions is in the corpus and deserves a closer reading than this summary gives it. Who first used the dog and cart, and what it meant, remains disputed. Readers should treat the reconstructions of Chrysippus's exact views here as the scholars' reconstructions, not his words.$q$, 105, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/fate-providence-up-to-us.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
